   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "# 3.06 — Theorema Egregium\n\nGauss published this in 1827 and called it *egregium* — remarkable. It is the result the whole stage has been arranged around.\n\n> **Theorema Egregium.** The Gaussian curvature $K$ can be computed from the first fundamental form alone.\n\nTo see why that is startling, recall how $K$ was *defined* in 3.04:\n\n$$K = \\frac{LN - M^2}{EG - F^2},$$\n\nwhere $L, M, N$ come from $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$, which is built by dotting second derivatives of $\\sigma$ against the **unit normal** $n$. The normal points out of the surface into the surrounding space. Every ingredient of the numerator refers to the embedding.\n\nAnd yet the answer does not. $K$ turns out to be expressible in $E, F, G$ and their derivatives — quantities the ant of 3.03 can measure without ever suspecting there is an outside.\n\n**Consequences, in increasing order of importance:**\n\n- **The cylinder's $K = 0$ was forced.** 3.03 showed the cylinder is intrinsically a plane; 3.04 computed $K = 0$ for both. That is now a theorem, not a coincidence.\n- **The sphere cannot be flattened.** A plane has $K = 0$, a sphere $K = 1/R^2 \\ne 0$; since $K$ is intrinsic, no isometry between them can exist. Every flat map of the Earth must distort — proved, not merely observed as in 3.03.\n- **Curvature does not need an ambient space.** This is the conceptual payload. If $K$ is intrinsic, then a curved geometry can be defined *without* embedding it in anything. Riemann took exactly this step in 1854, and Stage 4 follows him.\n\nThe machinery needed is the **Christoffel symbols**, which express the second derivatives of $\\sigma$ in terms of the moving frame $\\{\\sigma_u, \\sigma_v, n\\}$ — and which, remarkably, depend only on $E, F, G$.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 1. Christoffel symbols\n\nAt each point $\\{\\sigma_u, \\sigma_v, n\\}$ is a basis of $\\mathbb R^3$ — not orthonormal, but a basis. So every second derivative of $\\sigma$ can be expanded in it:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\sigma_{uu} &= \\Gamma^1_{11}\\sigma_u + \\Gamma^2_{11}\\sigma_v + L\\,n,\\\\\n\\sigma_{uv} &= \\Gamma^1_{12}\\sigma_u + \\Gamma^2_{12}\\sigma_v + M\\,n,\\\\\n\\sigma_{vv} &= \\Gamma^1_{22}\\sigma_u + \\Gamma^2_{22}\\sigma_v + N\\,n.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nThe normal components are exactly $L, M, N$ — the second fundamental form, by definition. The **tangential** components are the **Christoffel symbols** $\\Gamma^k_{ij}$, and the crucial fact is:\n\n> **The Christoffel symbols depend only on $E, F, G$.**\n\nThe reason is short. Dotting the equations above with $\\sigma_u$ and $\\sigma_v$ kills the $n$ terms and leaves a linear system whose right-hand sides are things like $\\sigma_{uu}\\cdot\\sigma_u = \\tfrac12 E_u$ — derivatives of the first fundamental form. Solving gives, in the general index notation of Stage 4,\n\n$$\\Gamma^k_{ij} = \\tfrac12\\,g^{kl}\\bigl(\\partial_i g_{jl} + \\partial_j g_{il} - \\partial_l g_{ij}\\bigr),$$\n\nwith $g_{ij} = \\mathrm{I}$. Nothing extrinsic appears.\n\n## 2. The Gauss equation\n\nNow use equality of mixed partials. The vector $\\sigma$ is smooth, so\n\n$$(\\sigma_{uu})_v = (\\sigma_{uv})_u .$$\n\nExpand both sides using the expressions above, and collect the coefficient of $\\sigma_v$. The left side contributes terms in $\\Gamma$'s and their derivatives, plus a term $L\\,n_v$; the right side similarly, with $M\\,n_u$. The normal derivatives $n_u, n_v$ are themselves tangential (3.04), and expressing them via the shape operator produces $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ terms.\n\nWhat emerges after the dust settles is the **Gauss equation**, whose content is\n\n$$\\underbrace{LN - M^2}_{\\text{extrinsic}} \\;=\\; \\underbrace{\\text{an expression in } E, F, G \\text{ and their derivatives}}_{\\text{intrinsic}} .$$\n\nDividing by $EG - F^2$ gives $K$ intrinsically. $\\blacksquare$\n\n**In the orthogonal case** ($F = 0$), which covers every example in this journey, the result reduces to a formula one can actually use:\n\n$$K = -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{EG}}\\left[ \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial u}\\!\\left(\\frac{G_u}{\\sqrt{EG}}\\right) + \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial v}\\!\\left(\\frac{E_v}{\\sqrt{EG}}\\right) \\right].$$\n\nOnly $E$ and $G$ appear. We verify below that this agrees with $\\det\\mathrm{I\\!I}/\\det\\mathrm{I}$ on every surface from 3.04–3.05 — which is the theorem, checked case by case.",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"linearalgebra\")$\nratprint : false$\n\ncross3(a,b) := [a[2]*b[3]-a[3]*b[2], a[3]*b[1]-a[1]*b[3], a[1]*b[2]-a[2]*b[1]]$\npd(s,var)   := map(lambda([e], diff(e, var)), s)$\nts(e)       := trigsimp(e)$\n\nFFF(s,u_,v_) := block([su,sv], su : pd(s,u_), sv : pd(s,v_),\n  matrix([ts(su.su), ts(su.sv)], [ts(su.sv), ts(sv.sv)]))$\nunitnormal(s,u_,v_) := block([n], n : cross3(pd(s,u_), pd(s,v_)),\n  map(ts, n/sqrt(ts(n . n))))$\nSFF(s,u_,v_) := block([nn,suu,suv,svv],\n  nn : unitnormal(s,u_,v_),\n  suu : pd(pd(s,u_),u_), suv : pd(pd(s,u_),v_), svv : pd(pd(s,v_),v_),\n  matrix([ts(suu.nn), ts(suv.nn)], [ts(suv.nn), ts(svv.nn)]))$\n\n/* K the EXTRINSIC way: det II / det I — needs the normal, hence the embedding */\nK_extrinsic(s,u_,v_) := ts( determinant(SFF(s,u_,v_))/determinant(FFF(s,u_,v_)) )$\n\n/* K the INTRINSIC way: from E and G alone, valid when F = 0 */\nK_intrinsic(s,u_,v_) := block([I1, E_, G_, W],\n  I1 : FFF(s,u_,v_),\n  if ts(I1[1][2]) # 0 then error(\"this formula assumes F = 0\"),\n  E_ : I1[1][1],  G_ : I1[2][2],\n  W  : sqrt(E_*G_),\n  ts( -1/(2*W) * ( diff( diff(G_,u_)/W, u_ ) + diff( diff(E_,v_)/W, v_ ) ) ) )$\n\nassume(R > 0, a > 0, b > 0, u > 0, u < %pi)$\nsphere   : [R*sin(u)*cos(v), R*sin(u)*sin(v), R*cos(u)]$\ncylinder : [R*cos(u), R*sin(u), v]$\nplane    : [u, v, 0]$\ncatenoid : [cosh(u)*cos(v), cosh(u)*sin(v), u]$\n\nprint(\"   surface     K extrinsic (det II/det I)   K intrinsic (E, G only)   equal?\")$\nfor su in [[\"plane   \", plane], [\"cylinder\", cylinder],\n           [\"sphere  \", sphere], [\"catenoid\", catenoid]] do\n  block([ke, ki],\n    ke : K_extrinsic(su[2],u,v),\n    ki : K_intrinsic(su[2],u,v),\n    print(\"   \", su[1], \"      \", ke, \"                \", ki,\n          \"        \", is( ratsimp(ke - ki) = 0 )) )$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The left column needed the unit normal — the surrounding space.\")$\nprint(\"The right column needed only E and G — measurable from inside.\")$\nprint(\"They agree. That is the Theorema Egregium ∎\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 1,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "   surface     K extrinsic (det II/det I)   K intrinsic (E, G only)   equal?\n    plane           0                  0          true\n    cylinder        0                  0          true\n    sphere          1/R^2                  1/R^2          true\n    catenoid        -(1/cosh(u)^4)                  -(1/cosh(u)^4)\n   true\nThe left column needed the unit normal — the surrounding space.\nThe right column needed only E and G — measurable from inside.\nThey agree. That is the Theorema Egregium ∎"
     ]
    }
   ]
