  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## 3. The sharpest test: the catenoid and the helicoid\n\nA verification surface-by-surface is convincing but not decisive — one could imagine $K$ agreeing by accident. The real test is to find two surfaces that are **manifestly different in space** yet have the **same first fundamental form**, and check that $K$ agrees while $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ does not.\n\nThe classic pair:\n\n$$\\text{catenoid}\\;(\\cosh u\\cos v,\\;\\cosh u \\sin v,\\; u), \\qquad \\text{helicoid}\\;(w\\cos v,\\; w\\sin v,\\; v).$$\n\nOne is a surface of revolution — the shape of a soap film between two rings. The other is a spiral ramp, swept by a horizontal line rising as it rotates. They are not remotely alike: the catenoid is rotationally symmetric and topologically an annulus; the helicoid is a screw and is simply connected.\n\nSubstituting $w = \\sinh \\tilde u$ in the helicoid makes the two first fundamental forms **identical**. So the surfaces are locally isometric: one can be bent into the other without stretching — and in fact there is a continuous family of minimal surfaces deforming the catenoid into the helicoid, each member isometric to the others.\n\nSince $K$ is intrinsic, it must be the same function on both. Since $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ is not, it need not be — and it isn't. This is the theorem in its sharpest form: **the first fundamental form determines $K$ but does not determine the surface.**",
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* Catenoid, and the helicoid reparametrised by w = sinh(u) */\ncatenoid : [cosh(u)*cos(v), cosh(u)*sin(v), u]$\nhelicoid : [sinh(u)*cos(v), sinh(u)*sin(v), v]$\n\nIc : FFF(catenoid, u, v)$\nIh : FFF(helicoid, u, v)$\nprint(\"catenoid  I  = \", Ic)$\nprint(\"helicoid  I  = \", Ih)$\nprint(\"identical?     \", is( ratsimp(Ic - Ih) = zeromatrix(2,2) ), \"  ⟹ locally isometric\")$\n\nIIc : SFF(catenoid, u, v)$\nIIh : SFF(helicoid, u, v)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"catenoid  II = \", IIc)$\nprint(\"helicoid  II = \", IIh)$\nprint(\"identical?     \", is( ratsimp(IIc - IIh) = zeromatrix(2,2) ),\n      \"  ⟹ they sit in space quite differently\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"catenoid  K  = \", K_extrinsic(catenoid,u,v))$\nprint(\"helicoid  K  = \", K_extrinsic(helicoid,u,v))$\nprint(\"equal?         \", is( ratsimp(K_extrinsic(catenoid,u,v)\n                                     - K_extrinsic(helicoid,u,v)) = 0 ), \"  ✓\")$\n\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"So: same I, different II, same K.\")$\nprint(\"K is determined by I — which is the theorem — while the surface is not.\")$\n\n/* Mean curvature: both are minimal, but that is a separate fact */\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"(both are also minimal: H = \",\n      ts( (Ic[1][1]*IIc[2][2] - 2*Ic[1][2]*IIc[1][2] + Ic[2][2]*IIc[1][1])\n          /(2*determinant(Ic)) ), \" and \",\n      ts( (Ih[1][1]*IIh[2][2] - 2*Ih[1][2]*IIh[1][2] + Ih[2][2]*IIh[1][1])\n          /(2*determinant(Ih)) ), \")\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 2,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "catenoid  I  =  matrix([cosh(u)^2,0],[0,cosh(u)^2])\nhelicoid  I  =  matrix([cosh(u)^2,0],[0,cosh(u)^2])\nidentical?      true   ⟹ locally isometric\ncatenoid  II =  matrix([-1,0],[0,1])\nhelicoid  II =  matrix([0,-1],[-1,0])\nidentical?      false   ⟹ they sit in space quite differently\ncatenoid  K  =  -(1/cosh(u)^4)\nhelicoid  K  =  -(1/cosh(u)^4)\nequal?          true   ✓\nSo: same I, different II, same K.\nK is determined by I — which is the theorem — while the surface is not.\n(both are also minimal: H =  0  and  0 )"
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "source": "/* --- Christoffel symbols from the METRIC ALONE --- */\n/* Γ^k_ij = ½ g^{kl} (∂_i g_jl + ∂_j g_il − ∂_l g_ij) */\nchristoffel(I1, vars) := block([ginv, G],\n  ginv : ratsimp(invert(I1)),\n  G : makelist( makelist( makelist(\n        ts( sum( ginv[k][l]*( diff(I1[j][l], vars[i])\n                            + diff(I1[i][l], vars[j])\n                            - diff(I1[i][j], vars[l]) ), l, 1, 2)/2 ),\n        j, 1, 2), i, 1, 2), k, 1, 2),\n  G )$\n\n/* --- Christoffel symbols from the EMBEDDING, as tangential components --- */\n/* Solve σ_ij = Γ¹ σ_u + Γ² σ_v + (II)_ij n  for the two tangential coefficients */\nchristoffel_embedded(s, u_, v_) := block([su, sv, I1, sij, rhs, sol, out],\n  su : pd(s,u_),  sv : pd(s,v_),  I1 : FFF(s,u_,v_),\n  out : makelist( makelist( makelist(0, j,1,2), i,1,2), k,1,2),\n  for i : 1 thru 2 do\n    for j : 1 thru 2 do block([d1, d2, cc],\n      sij : pd( pd(s, if i=1 then u_ else v_), if j=1 then u_ else v_ ),\n      /* project onto the tangent plane: solve I1 · c = (σ_ij·σ_u, σ_ij·σ_v) */\n      rhs : [ ts(sij . su), ts(sij . sv) ],\n      cc  : ratsimp( invert(I1) . transpose(matrix(rhs)) ),\n      out[1][i][j] : ts(cc[1][1]),\n      out[2][i][j] : ts(cc[2][1]) ),\n  out )$\n\n/* Compare on the sphere */\nIsph : FFF(sphere, u, v)$\nGm : christoffel(Isph, [u, v])$\nGe : christoffel_embedded(sphere, u, v)$\nprint(\"sphere, I = \", Isph)$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"Christoffel symbols from the metric alone:\")$\nprint(\"   Γ¹ = \", Gm[1], \"     Γ² = \", Gm[2])$\nprint(\"from the embedding (tangential parts of σ_ij):\")$\nprint(\"   Γ¹ = \", Ge[1], \"     Γ² = \", Ge[2])$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"difference: \", ratsimp(Gm[1] - Ge[1]), \" and \", ratsimp(Gm[2] - Ge[2]))$\nprint(\"\")$\nprint(\"The metric formula never mentions the normal, yet reproduces the\")$\nprint(\"tangential parts of the second derivatives exactly. That is why the\")$\nprint(\"Gauss equation can express an extrinsic quantity intrinsically.\")$",
   "metadata": {},
   "execution_count": 3,
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "sphere, I =  matrix([R^2,0],[0,R^2*sin(u)^2])\nChristoffel symbols from the metric alone:\n   Γ¹ =  [[0,0],[0,-(cos(u)*sin(u))]]      Γ² =\n        [[0,cos(u)/sin(u)],[cos(u)/sin(u),0]]\nfrom the embedding (tangential parts of σ_ij):\n   Γ¹ =  [[0,0],[0,-(cos(u)*sin(u))]]      Γ² =\n        [[0,cos(u)/sin(u)],[cos(u)/sin(u),0]]\ndifference:  [[0,0],[0,0]]  and  [[0,0],[0,0]]\nThe metric formula never mentions the normal, yet reproduces the\ntangential parts of the second derivatives exactly. That is why the\nGauss equation can express an extrinsic quantity intrinsically."
     ]
