  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "source": "## Summary\n\n$$K = \\frac{LN-M^2}{EG-F^2} \\quad\\text{(extrinsic definition)} \\qquad = \\qquad -\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{EG}}\\left[\\partial_u\\!\\left(\\frac{G_u}{\\sqrt{EG}}\\right) + \\partial_v\\!\\left(\\frac{E_v}{\\sqrt{EG}}\\right)\\right] \\quad\\text{(intrinsic, }F=0).$$\n\nVerified equal on the plane, cylinder, sphere and catenoid. The left-hand side needs the unit normal and hence the ambient space; the right-hand side needs only $E$ and $G$.\n\nThe decisive check was the **catenoid and helicoid**:\n\n| | catenoid | helicoid |\n|---|---|---|\n| $\\mathrm{I}$ | $\\mathrm{diag}(\\cosh^2 u, \\cosh^2 u)$ | **identical** |\n| $\\mathrm{I\\!I}$ | $\\mathrm{diag}(-1, 1)$ | $\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\-1&0\\end{pmatrix}$ — different |\n| $K$ | $-1/\\cosh^4 u$ | **identical** |\n| $H$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n\nTwo surfaces one cannot mistake for each other — a soap film between rings, and a spiral ramp — share a first fundamental form exactly, differ in their second, and have the same Gaussian curvature. **$\\mathrm{I}$ determines $K$; it does not determine the surface.**\n\nThe Christoffel symbols were computed twice: from $g_{ij}$ by the formula $\\Gamma^k_{ij} = \\tfrac12 g^{kl}(\\partial_i g_{jl} + \\partial_j g_{il} - \\partial_l g_{ij})$, and as the tangential parts of $\\sigma_{ij}$. They agree — $\\Gamma^1_{22} = -\\sin u\\cos u$ and $\\Gamma^2_{12} = \\cot u$ on the sphere — and the first computation never mentions the normal.\n\n## Why this changes everything\n\nUp to this point, curvature has been *of a surface sitting in space*. The Theorema Egregium severs that dependence. If $K$ can be computed from a metric, then a metric is all one needs — the surrounding space was scaffolding, and it can be removed.\n\nThat is precisely the step Riemann took in his 1854 *Habilitationsvortrag*: define a geometry by specifying $g_{ij}$ on a coordinate patch, with no ambient space at all, in any number of dimensions. Everything in Stage 4 is downstream of it:\n\n| Here (surface in $\\mathbb R^3$) | Stage 4 (abstract manifold) |\n|---|---|\n| $E, F, G$ | the metric $g_{ij}$ |\n| $\\Gamma^k_{ij}$ from $E,F,G$ | the Levi-Civita connection (4.02) |\n| Gauss equation | the Riemann curvature tensor (4.04) |\n| $K$ | sectional curvature (4.05) |\n| $\\sqrt{EG-F^2}$ | the volume element $\\sqrt{\\det g}$ |\n\nAnd the physical payoff, four stages later: general relativity describes a curved spacetime with **no** ambient space to be curved *in*. That is only coherent because curvature is intrinsic, which is what Gauss proved here.\n\n## Next\n\n**3.07 — Geodesics on Surfaces** asks what \"straight\" means once the surface is curved. The answer is written entirely in Christoffel symbols — hence entirely intrinsically — and produces a nonlinear ODE system we integrate numerically.\n\n## References\n\n- do Carmo, M.P. (1976) *Differential Geometry of Curves and Surfaces*. Englewood Cliffs: Prentice-Hall. §4-3 contains the Gauss equation and the Theorema Egregium, with the full derivation compressed in §2 above.\n- Pressley, A. (2010) *Elementary Differential Geometry*. 2nd edn. Springer Undergraduate Mathematics Series. London: Springer. Chapter 10, which states the intrinsic formula for $K$ in the orthogonal case used here.\n- ★ Lee, J.M. (2018) *Introduction to Riemannian Manifolds*. Cham: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-3-319-91755-9. Chapter 8 proves the Gauss equation in the general submanifold setting; Chapter 7 develops curvature intrinsically from the start.\n- ★ Callahan, J.J. (2000) *The Geometry of Spacetime*. New York: Springer. doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-6736-0. Chapter 5–6, tracing exactly the route from surfaces to intrinsic curvature to relativity.\n- Gauss, C.F. (1827) *Disquisitiones generales circa superficies curvas*. Göttingen. The original, in which the theorem appears as Article 12 and earns its name.\n- Riemann, B. (1854) *Über die Hypothesen, welche der Geometrie zu Grunde liegen*. The lecture that took Gauss's result and removed the ambient space entirely.\n\n**A note on scope.** The full derivation of the Gauss equation is a page of index bookkeeping, and §2 summarises rather than performs it. What is *verified* here is the conclusion — that the two formulas for $K$ agree — on four surfaces plus the catenoid–helicoid pair. Readers wanting the derivation line by line should see do Carmo §4-3.\n\n---\n*Marked ★ = consulted directly while writing; the rest are standard references given from knowledge, not consulted here. Every code cell in this notebook was executed and its output is the real result; the prose is written from knowledge and checked against these sources where they cover the material.*",
   "metadata": {}
  }
 ]
